/// Phase B non-escape enforcement for `inout(name) : T` (per
/// `plans/MEMORY_SAFETY.md`).
///
/// A `inout(name) : T` parameter is a second-class reference to
/// the caller's storage — it lowers to `T*` at the calling
/// convention level. A closure capturing a ref-param would
/// outlive the call frame (stored in a struct, returned, sent to a
/// Future) and dereference a dangling pointer. The evaluator rejects
/// every such capture; the negatives here keep the gate intact.
///
/// Other escape vectors (`r := ref_param` binding, struct field
/// containing a ref binding) are structurally impossible: there's
/// no syntax for a "ref type", only a `ref` *parameter
/// modifier*. So a let-binding copies the value (fine) and a struct
/// field can't name the binding.
// Closure that reads a ref parameter is rejected.
comptime_expect_error(
  make_reader :: (fn(inout(x) : i32) -> Impl(Fn() -> i32))(
    () => x
  )
);
// Closure that writes a ref parameter is rejected.
comptime_expect_error(
  make_writer :: (fn(inout(x) : i32) -> Impl(Fn() -> unit))(() => {
    x = i32(99);
  })
);
// Nested closure capturing an outer ref param is rejected.
// Two closure layers: outer `=>` captures nothing ref; inner `=>`
// captures the ref `x`. The gate fires on the inner closure.
comptime_expect_error(
  build :: (fn(inout(x) : i32) -> Impl(Fn() -> Impl(Fn() -> i32)))(
    () => (() => x)
  )
);
// Positive guardrail: a closure that does NOT touch any ref
// parameter still compiles cleanly, even when the enclosing fn
// takes a ref. The returned closure only captures the local
// `y_copy`, not `x`.
no_capture :: (fn(inout(x) : i32, y : i32) -> Impl(Fn() -> i32))({
  y_copy := y;
  () => y_copy
});
test("ref-param closure capture gate doesn't false-positive on uncaptured ref", {
  v := i32(5);
  closure := no_capture(v, i32(42));
  assert(closure() == i32(42), "y_copy returned");
});
